//! lotus/stereo_limit_env.rye -- ALES163: the ATTACK/RELEASE BRICKWALL LIMITER carried into stereo -- the LINKED envelope (ALES160) driving ONE LINKED ceiling (ALES158) over time. ALES158's stereo_limit pins a pair the instant
//! it crosses the ceiling: the peak lands exactly on the ceiling the moment it arrives, and the pinning vanishes the moment the pair falls. A real limiter breathes -- it approaches the ceiling over an attack and releases the
//! gain gently afterward. ALES160 built exactly that breath for stereo (one shared envelope tracking the linked peak) and proved it once; ALES161 spent it for the compressor, ALES162 for the gate. This rung spends the same
//! proof for the LIMITER: it drives the gain reduction from the smoothed LINKED envelope rather than the bare pair, and applies the ONE resulting gain to both channels -- the compressor at an infinite ratio, the envelope's
//! target pinned exactly to the ceiling, the image held to the sample the whole time.
//!
//! Two proofs fused. The DETECTOR is linked and time-smoothed (ALES160): the one envelope steps toward max(|left|, |right|) clamped to the rail, the louder channel deciding the shared loudness over time. The GAIN is linked
//! (ALES158): where the smoothed envelope crosses the ceiling the single gain ceil/e (in (0, 1) since 1 <= ceil < e) is applied to BOTH raw samples. Limiting each channel by its own envelope would reduce on different samples
//! and shift the image; one detector and one gain keep the field aligned.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadCeiling (outside [1, sample_max]), BadCoeff (an attack or release outside (0, 1]), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any write. Otherwise walk the span one sample-PAIR at
//! a time, advancing ONE i64 envelope toward the linked peak by ALES160's stereo_env_step: envelope within the ceiling leaves the pair playing (both pass byte for byte); envelope above the ceiling applies the one gain ceil/e
//! to both raw samples. On the above-ceiling branch e > ceil >= 1, so the divide is always safe -- the zero-envelope case the gate faced cannot arise here.
//!
//! The crux -- six laws. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW (the crux link): unit attack and unit release make the linked envelope the instantaneous linked peak, so this equals ALES158's memoryless stereo_limit byte for byte on both -- the
//! time-varying rung's zero-smoothing limit is exactly the memoryless rung already proven. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' equal to ALES55's mono limit_env(left) byte for byte, the right
//! scaled by the same fraction ceil/e, the independent per-channel limit_env genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase, no magnitude
//! expanding, no sign flipping. THE KNOB / NO-LOOKAHEAD LAW: below the ceiling the identity, a slower attack letting a fast transient briefly overshoot the ceiling before the envelope catches it (a gain-reducer over time, not
//! a sample-clamp -- monotone), an instant attack pinning the peak-holding channel exactly on the ceiling. THE CARRY LAW: a span limited in two pieces equals the whole once on both. THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE /
//! ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked envelope never crosses the ceiling passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and
//! balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES163.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-070439_fill-ales163-lotus-stereo-limit-env.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_envelope.rye (ALES160) -- the LINKED envelope, its stereo_env_step advancing the one shared detector toward the linked peak, reused whole; lotus/stereo_limit.rye (ALES158) -- the LINKED ceiling this
//! drives over time, its instantaneous form the unit-coefficient limit re-proven beside it; lotus/limit_env.rye (ALES55) -- the mono attack/release limiter, its LimitEnvError reused whole and its output the left-always-peak
//! comparison; lotus/limit.rye (ALES49) -- the hard ceiling; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope, siloed to lotus/. One linked-peak comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide for the
//! envelope step plus one per channel for the gain, all in i64 (|sample|-ceil <= 32767-32767 never overflowing), the gain ceil/e in (0, 1) so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's and fits i16. The ceiling is a
//! magnitude in sample units, the attack and release fractions per sample index rather than milliseconds against a clock. NO LOOKAHEAD -- a slow attack lets a fast transient briefly overshoot before the envelope catches it (a
//! true brickwall that never crosses under any attack wants a lookahead delay, a horizon rung); no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_limit_env.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const stereo_envelope = @import("stereo_envelope.rye");
const stereo_limit = @import("stereo_limit.rye");
const limit_env = @import("limit_env.rye");
const limit = @import("limit.rye");

pub const stereo_limit_env_version = "20260815.070439";

/// The linked attack/release limiter's faults are exactly ALES55's own `LimitEnvError` -- `BadCeiling`, `BadCoeff`, `BadRange` -- reused by name, since fusing the linked detector with the linked ceiling adds no new fault: it
/// validates one shared ceiling, one shared attack, one shared release, and one span, then advances a single envelope and applies one gain per pair to both channels together.
pub const StereoLimitEnvError = limit_env.LimitEnvError;

/// Limit `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc` to the magnitude `ceil`, the ONE gain reduction FOLLOWING the LINKED envelope that CONTINUES from the caller's shared `env` (carried-state, ALES43's
/// lesson). Each pair advances ALES160's linked follower one step toward the linked peak `max(|left|, |right|)` (the attack fraction while rising, release while falling); where the smoothed envelope sits within the ceiling both
/// channels pass byte for byte, and where it rises above, the single gain `ceil/e` (in (0, 1)) is applied to BOTH raw samples -- ALES49's ceiling reached over time on the linked envelope rather than clamped the instant it is
/// crossed. The caller owns the i64 envelope across calls, so a span limited in two pieces equals the whole once. Refuses BadCeiling, BadCoeff, and BadRange before any write; on refusal `env` and both channels are untouched.
/// Values only -- never the length, and the channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_limit_follow_carry(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
) StereoLimitEnvError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the ceiling is a legal magnitude -- at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] -- checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length -- checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never limits one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const cl: i64 = @as(i64, ceil);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the ONE shared linked follower toward the pair's linked peak (ALES160's proven step); e is the smoothed linked magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = stereo_envelope.stereo_env_step(env, xl, xr, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the linked envelope stays a legal magnitude -- never negative, never past the rail (the follower's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e > cl) {
            // The smoothed linked detector has crossed the ceiling; the single gain is ceil/e, in (0, 1) since 1 <= ceil < e. e > cl >= 1, so the divide is always safe (the zero-envelope case the gate faced cannot arise here).
            const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
            const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
            // ONE shared gain ceil/e across both channels: m-ceil <= 32768-32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps each quotient strictly below its m (ceil < e).
            const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * cl, e);
            const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * cl, e);
            // invariant: the shared gain reduces, never boosts, and never below zero, on each channel -- ceil < e makes ceil/e < 1, so a smoothed limiter only ever leans down.
            assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nr <= mr and nr >= 0);
            const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ml <= sample_max
            const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
            sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
            sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
            // invariant: each written sample holds its input's sign (magnitude only shrank), zero being sign-neutral.
            assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
            assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
        }
        // envelope within the ceiling: the pair plays -- both left exactly as they were (the gain reduction has not opened).
    }

    // invariant: a limiter changes sample values only -- each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

/// Limit `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc`, the one gain reduction following a LINKED envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh shared env of 0) -- one implementation shared with the carried
/// form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses BadCeiling, BadCoeff, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn stereo_limit_follow(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
) StereoLimitEnvError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    return stereo_limit_follow_carry(sc, start, count, &env, ceil, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den);
}

// --- selftest: THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW -- unit attack and release reduce it to ALES158's memoryless stereo_limit byte for byte on both; THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES55's mono
//     limit_env(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent reduction differing; THE IMAGE LAW -- no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase; THE KNOB / NO-LOOKAHEAD
//     LAW -- below the ceiling the identity, a slower attack letting the transient overshoot; THE CARRY LAW -- a span in two pieces equals the whole once on both; THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW -- the crux link. With unit attack AND unit release the linked envelope IS the instantaneous linked peak (ALES160's unit identity), so the gain application lands each pair at exactly ALES158's
    //    memoryless stereo_limit output. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same master and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var smoothed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&smoothed, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&smoothed, 0, 8, 1500, 1, 1, 1, 1); // instant attack, instant release
        var plain = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&plain, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit.stereo_limit(&plain, 0, 8, 1500); // the proven ALES158 memoryless linked limiter
        if (!eq(smoothed.left.samples(), plain.left.samples())) return error.UnitLeftNotMemoryless;
        if (!eq(smoothed.right.samples(), plain.right.samples())) return error.UnitRightNotMemoryless;
    }

    // 2. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere) makes the shared envelope equal the mono envelope of the left, so left' equals ALES55's
    //    mono limit_env(left) byte for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction ceil/e -- a different, image-preserving result than limiting the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 500, -2000, 6000, -300, 6000, -6000 }; // |left| >= |right| everywhere; the left holds the linked peak
        const rr = [_]i16{ 2000, -1000, 100, -1500, 700, -60, 1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&sc, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5); // instant attack, release 1:5

        // the peak-holding left equals ALES55's mono limit_env of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try limit_env.limit_follow(&mono_l, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;

        // the INDEPENDENT reduction genuinely differs -- mono limit_env of the right alone follows the right's own (quieter) envelope, scaling on a different curve, an image-shifting result.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try limit_env.limit_follow(&mono_r, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5);
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW -- the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase pair (right = −left) comes back STILL out of phase, both
    //    scaled by the identical time-smoothed fraction.
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000 };
        const ir = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -3000, 200, -700, 700 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 6);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 6) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }

        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 6000, -8000, 12000, -4000 }, &[_]i16{ -6000, 8000, -12000, 4000 });
        try stereo_limit_follow(&op, 0, 4, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 6);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
    }

    // 4a. BELOW THE CEILING IS THE IDENTITY on both -- a master whose every linked peak sits within the ceiling never drives the envelope above it, so the whole master passes byte for byte regardless of smoothing.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 500, -900, 1000, -1000, 250, -500 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -700, 200, -50, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8); // ceiling at the loudest peak — the envelope never exceeds it
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.SubCeilingNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4b. A SLOWER ATTACK LETS THE TRANSIENT THROUGH -- on a sudden loud onset after quiet, a slower attack reduces the first above-ceiling pairs LESS than an instant attack (the gain reduction lags the linked peak -- the
    //     honest overshoot of a limiter without lookahead). So the slow-attack magnitude is no smaller than the instant-attack magnitude at every sample on the left, and the first onset sample is strictly louder under the slow
    //     attack; the instant attack pins the peak-holding left exactly on the ceiling.
    {
        const ol = [_]i16{ 100, 100, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000 }; // quiet, then a hard onset on the left (it holds the linked peak)
        const orr = [_]i16{ -50, 50, -3000, 3000, -3000, 3000, -3000, 3000 };
        const ceil: i32 = 1500;
        var slow = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&slow, &ol, &orr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&slow, 0, 8, ceil, 1, 8, 1, 8); // slow attack 1:8
        var fast = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&fast, &ol, &orr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&fast, 0, 8, ceil, 1, 1, 1, 8); // instant attack 1:1
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 8) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(slow.left.samples()[i]) < abs16(fast.left.samples()[i])) return error.SlowAttackClampedMore;
        }
        if (abs16(slow.left.samples()[2]) <= abs16(fast.left.samples()[2])) return error.TransientDidNotSurvive;
        // the instant-attack onset sample lands exactly on the ceiling (the peak-holding left pinned there from within).
        if (abs16(fast.left.samples()[2]) != ceil) return error.InstantNotOnCeiling;
    }

    // 5. THE CARRY LAW -- limiting a span in two pieces (the second continuing the first's ending shared envelope) equals limiting the whole span once, byte for byte on BOTH channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 2000, -14000, 14000, -2000, 9000, -9000, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 6000, -1000, 2000, -700, 800, -1500, 1500 };
        var whole = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&whole, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_follow(&whole, 0, 8, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5);

        var split = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&split, &ll, &rr);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        try stereo_limit_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5); // first piece
        try stereo_limit_follow_carry(&split, 3, 5, &env, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5); // second continues the shared state
        if (!eq(split.left.samples(), whole.left.samples())) return error.CarryLeftNotEqual;
        if (!eq(split.right.samples(), whole.right.samples())) return error.CarryRightNotEqual;
    }

    // 6a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master limits to zero on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_limit_follow(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, -50 });
        try stereo_limit_follow(&bc, 0, 5, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 6b. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a zero ceiling, an over-rail ceiling, an illegal coefficient, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_follow(&a, 0, 3, 0, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.ZeroCeilSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroCeil;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ZeroCeilRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_follow(&b, 0, 3, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.OverRailSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverRail;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.OverRailRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_follow(&c, 0, 3, 1500, 5, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.AttackRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_follow(&d, 1, 3, 1500, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 6c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to limit.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_limit_follow(&noop, 1, 0, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    // silence the unused import in builds that trim it -- limit is named in the doc lineage; touch it so the reference stays honest.
    _ = limit.limit_version;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-limit-env: the ATTACK/RELEASE BRICKWALL LIMITER CARRIED INTO STEREO — the LINKED envelope driving ONE LINKED ceiling over time. Limit a span of sample-PAIRS in a master to a magnitude ceiling, " ++
        "the one gain reduction following the shared linked envelope (max(|left|, |right|), attack while rising, release while falling): within the ceiling both pass byte for byte, above it the single gain ceil/e (in (0, 1)) " ++
        "is applied to both raw samples — the compressor at an infinite ratio, the target pinned to the ceiling. ALES158's memoryless stereo_limit pins a pair the instant it crosses; ALES160 built the linked breath; this " ++
        "fuses them — one detector, one gain, the image held. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW (the crux link): unit attack and release make the linked envelope the instantaneous linked peak, so this equals ALES158's memoryless " ++
        "stereo_limit byte for byte on both. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES55's mono limit_env(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent per-channel limit_env " ++
        "genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE KNOB / NO-LOOKAHEAD LAW: below the ceiling the " ++
        "identity, a slower attack letting a fast transient overshoot the ceiling before the envelope catches it (a gain-reducer over time, not a sample-clamp — monotone), an instant attack pinning the peak-holding channel " ++
        "exactly on the ceiling. THE CARRY LAW: a span limited in two pieces equals the whole once on both. THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked envelope never crosses the " ++
        "ceiling passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded " ++
        "i16 Clips carrying one shared i64 envelope, one linked-peak comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide for the step plus one per channel for the gain (in i64, no overflow), the gain ceil/e in (0, 1); the attack " ++
        "and release fractions per sample index; no lookahead; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
